WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.940
No matter where they live, the Elves sure
know how to decorate.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.629
So what's so important about the Elvish realm
of Lindon in Amazon's "The Rings of Power"?

00:00:08.771 --> 00:00:10.170
Now that "The Lord of the Rings: The Rings

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.980
of Power" is officially out, audiences are
seeing several Middle-earth regions through

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.550
a time-traveling lens, including Lindon.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.120
The Elvish nation technically shows up in
"The Lord of the Rings" for a short sequence

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.840
at the very end of the story, when Frodo and
Bilbo hop on a ship at the Grey Havens.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.830
But by that point, the area is a skeletal
remnant of the powerful Faerie kingdom that

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.560
exists there during "The Rings of Power" narrative
— which is set during the Second Age of

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.040
Middle-earth history.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.949
During that story, the elf-capital Lindon
is a powerhouse.

00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:40.030
Lindon is one of the last major strongholds
of Elven glory before the Elder Children of

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.999
Ilúvatar fade away, replaced by the young,
up-and-coming race of Men.

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:49.549
"The time has come for the dominion of men."

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:53.149
Here's a quick breakdown of the Elven Kingdom
and the crucial part that it plays in bridging

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:58.109
the gap between the epic Elder Days and "The
Lord of the Rings" story that fans know so well.

00:00:58.526 --> 00:01:02.920
Technically speaking, Lindon doesn't exist
as a unified and structured kingdom until

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.309
the Second Age begins.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:08.411
However, Elves live for a very long time,
and quite a few live in the area before it

00:01:08.411 --> 00:01:09.650
becomes a proper kingdom.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:13.610
"The Silmarillion" explains that the region
is initially called Ossiriand.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.360
The Green-elves dwell there, living secluded
lives.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.580
It reads,
"They were clad in green in spring and summer,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.310
and the sounds of their singing could be heard
even across the waters of Gelion; wherefore

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.170
the Noldor named that country Lindon, the
land of music —"

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.705
"I hear it said that when you cross over,
you hear a song.

00:01:30.705 --> 00:01:32.810
One whose memory we all carry."

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:38.270
When the First Age ends, the land to the west
of Lindon sinks in a catastrophic, world-breaking

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.160
event called the War of Wrath.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.930
In the process, Lindon becomes the farthest
western point on the Middle-earth map.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.370
Lindon is a major source of Elvish power during
the Second Age.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:52.380
There are other areas where Elvish strongholds
pop up, but none of them rival Lindon.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.460
Its leader is a fellow named Gil-galad.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.280
He's the fourth and last "High King" of a
famous group of Elves called the Noldor.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.430
His herald is a character that fans of "The
Lord of the Rings" are quite familiar with

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.980
— a Half-elven dude named Elrond.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.170
When Lindon is founded, Elrond is eons away
from his future as a wise and mighty counselor

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.920
and the leader of Rivendell.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.599
At this point, he's a very young Elf burdened
by incredible ancestry trying to find his

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:14.620
way in the world.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.260
A couple of other important Elves spend time
in Lindon with these two key leaders.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.659
The first is Galadriel, who may have lived
there before she left, leading Elves east

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:23.659
toward the Misty Mountains.

00:02:23.659 --> 00:02:28.541
In "The Rings of Power" storyline, Galadriel
is more integrated into Lindon, serving, when

00:02:28.541 --> 00:02:30.999
the show starts, as the Commander of its Northern
Armies.

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:35.319
The final Elf we can't fail to mention here
is Círdan, a famous shipbuilder who spends

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:39.380
his days helping his world-weary people leave
Middle-earth to set sail for the Blessed Realm

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.991
away in the West.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.540
The first rise of Sauron defines the Second
Age of Middle-earth history.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.650
In the Third Age that follows, Sauron re-emerges,
hunts for his Ring, and is ultimately toppled

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.696
by a Hobbit, a scrappy king in exile, and
a few other pretty important folks.

00:02:53.696 --> 00:02:55.037
"Keep breathing.

00:02:55.037 --> 00:02:57.717
That's the key.

00:02:57.717 --> 00:02:58.769
Breathe."

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.150
In the Second Age, though, Sauron is just
starting to settle into his role.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:06.409
The first thing he does is dress up in a handsome
physical form and call himself "Annatar, Lord

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:07.409
of Gifts."

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:10.760
He tries to dupe the Elves into thinking he's
a good guy that can help them.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.620
This is how he ends up making a bunch of Rings
of Power with some of the Elves.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.260
Before that happens, the folks in Lindon try
to stop him.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.180
"The Silmarillion" explains,
"Only to Lindon he did not come, for Gil-galad

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.370
and Elrond doubted him and his fair-seeming,
and though they knew not who in truth he was

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.090
they would not admit him to that land."

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.519
Gil-galad and Elrond continue to doubt Sauron
in disguise until matters finally come to

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:33.606
a head about halfway through the Second Age.

00:03:34.341 --> 00:03:38.790
Eventually, the secret gets out that Annatar
is Sauron after he, you know, makes the One

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.680
Ring and tries to control them directly and
stuff.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.569
When that happens, war erupts, and Sauron
goes on the offensive.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:48.959
Gil-galad lends Lindon's armies, under Elrond's
command, to resist Sauron's forces.

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:52.359
They fail, which leads to Elrond retreating
and founding Rivendell.

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:55.129
It's a setback, but Lindon itself isn't destroyed.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:59.999
On the contrary, it turns into one of the
key areas of Middle-earth that Sauron can't conquer.

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:03.450
Many Elves use Lindon's havens during this
time to hightail it out of Middle-earth entirely,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.329
but Gil-galad holds together enough of his
people to overcome the Dark Lord.

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:10.840
Over the rest of the Second Age, the fate
of Middle-earth swings back and forth.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.290
Lindon continues to play a key role, and Gil-galad
receives help from the Men of Númenor.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:19.620
The protracted, centuries-long drama ends
(temporarily, at least) when Gil-galad and

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.530
Elendil form the Last Alliance of Elves and
Men.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:27.020
The group gathers and heads down south, where
both leaders are killed, and Elendil's son,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.628
Isildur, cuts the One Ring from Sauron's hand.

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:33.620
After Sauron's first fall, the Third Age begins.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.570
Lindon still exists as an area where Elves
dwell, but its power is greatly diminished.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:42.050
So, while the area continues to be relevant
as an Elvish gateway from Middle-earth to

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.620
the Blessed Realm, otherwise its political
prowess is irrelevant.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.110
Its king is dead.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.990
Its new leader, Círdan, while a wise and
a masterful shipbuilder, isn't a High King

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.340
about to gather and lead the Elves to battle.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.552
It's worth noting, though, that some of the
Wizards come through his havens, and he even

00:04:56.552 --> 00:05:00.206
gives one of them an Elven Ring to help him
with his mission — Gandalf.

00:05:00.206 --> 00:05:04.036
"It's Gandalf!"

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:08.240
Even though there isn't much else going on,
the area continues to be distinctly important

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.660
— and very Elvish.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.590
The Hobbits, who settle down in the nearby
Shire halfway through the age, have stories

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:14.590
of the area.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:18.810
So, in the end, Lindon slowly fades into a
forgotten Elvish realm lost on the edge of

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.360
the Middle-earth story.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.680
But it doesn't change the fact that it plays
a prominent role in the Second Age — an

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.116
age that "The Rings of Power" series is about
to explore in detail.

